  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I finished the RBC-PHARM-07 review for Harold Vance (pt-0002). It led to two medication changes, and the pharmacy's screen turned out to be based on a wrong result.\n\n**Pharmacy screen used a mislabelled lab.** The pharmacist's note used a creatinine of 1.88 mg/dL from 10/21. That result is marked entered-in-error: the specimen was mislabelled and belongs to another patient. So the pharmacist's numbers (eGFR 35, CrCl 28) and suggested doses don't apply.\n\n**Values I used:**\n- **Creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL on 10/20, the most recent valid result.\n- **Weight:** 64.9 kg (143 lb), measured on 10/20. I skipped the 10/22 weight of 132 lb because it was his own phone estimate, not a measurement.\n- **Age:** 82.\n\n**Results by drug:**\n- **Apixaban:** only one of the three dose-reduction criteria applies now (age 80 or older). His weight is above 60 kg and his creatinine is below 1.5, though both met the cutoffs during the February admission. The policy dose is therefore 5 mg twice daily. I changed his 2.5 mg twice-daily order to 5 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0001).\n- **Metformin:** eGFR is 59.8, which is above the policy's cutoff of 45. No change.\n- **Gabapentin:** creatinine clearance is 43 mL/min. At that level the policy allows 400–1400 mg a day, given twice daily, and he was on 1800 mg a day split three times. I changed it to 600 mg twice daily, 1200 mg a day (new order mr-t0002).\n- **Other medications:** the policy doesn't cover his other medications, so I left them unchanged.\n\nYour progress note (doc-t0003) records the values, each rule's result and the changes, and cites the source results.\n\nI haven't told the patient or the pharmacy about the dose changes, and I didn't set up any follow-up tasks. The note suggests letting both know, watching his nerve pain on the lower gabapentin dose, and repeating creatinine and weight at his next routine visit.",
